{
  "title": "A game that ends without a winner leaves the match live, and its next game lands in the finished game's directory",
  "description": "MECHANISM, from source and from the corpus on disk. MatchImpl.endGame \
credits a win only `if (player.hasWon())`, and MatchImpl.checkIfMatchEnds ends the \
match only when some player's wins reach winsNeeded (or fewer than two players remain). \
A game that finishes with NOBODY marked as having won -- a deck-out, an effect, a concession, or a genuine draw; anything \
where neither seat is marked as having won -- therefore credits no win, the match is not over, and \
TableController:855 immediately calls startGame again. That next game inherits the \
match's gameLogDir, which is the directory of the game that just finished, so the \
server appends a second game's events into it while the sequential runner has already \
moved its observer to the next table. THE ENGINE IS BEHAVING CORRECTLY for a \
best-of-N match; what is wrong is a harness that plays one game per table and never \
tells the server the match is done. EVIDENCE. Step-1 corpus, 3,964 game directories, \
10 hold two games (0.25%). The two games never OVERLAP in time -- the second begins \
1.1s after the first ends in 8 of 10, 0.1s in one -- which is what ruled out a \
directory race between workers, since two racing writers overlap by definition. Every \
affected session produced 5 distinct sibling directories (_s1.._s5), so claim_game_dir \
worked in every case. The second game carries a THIRD game id, belonging to neither \
the finished game nor the next table's game, so it is not a misdirected write but a \
game nobody was watching. Nine of the ten first games ended with NO player at or below \
0 life, against 583 of 600 sampled normal games ending on a kill; three of the ten ended during the \
DRAW STEP, which is a deck-out and not a drawn game -- `step` is the turn step, not \
an outcome. NOT SUFFICIENT, ONLY NECESSARY: 12 of those 600 \
normal games also ended without a kill and did NOT double, which is consistent with \
the second game only appearing if it starts before the session tears the server down. \
So the rate is a function of timing, not of worker count -- it does not fall with \
--parallel and does not gate raising it. An earlier claim that it scaled with \
concurrency was mine and is withdrawn. FIX: the keepAlive observer now quits the \
previous match before creating the next table (quitPreviousMatch, tracking the watched \
game id), so the match ends with its game. The runner additionally reports any \
directory that received a second game at session end -- checked at the END because a \
check straight after game_end would pass, the second game arriving ~1.1s later. Loud \
rather than fatal: the games are already on disk and failing the session would not \
unwrite them; what matters is seeing it at generation rather than inferring it at \
assembly from a provenance field that is right for one of the two games. The detector \
was validated against the real corpus, not only fixtures: 3,964 directories scanned, \
exactly the 10 known cases found.",
